export const FR_C2_S02_ENGLISH: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "fr-c2-le-parapluie-en-cinq-manieres": [
    `First, here is the fact as it happened, without ornament. One Tuesday in November, on tram line B in Bordeaux, a man of about forty, seeing the doors closing, grabbed by mistake the black umbrella of the woman next to him, hanging from the same bar as his own and exactly identical, and got off at Grand Théâtre, leaving his own behind. Its owner, Mme Awa Diop, noticed, got off at the next stop with the stranger's umbrella, ran back through the downpour and caught up with him outside a bookshop on the cours de l'Intendance. He apologised. She laughed. They swapped umbrellas. That's all. Or almost. For a fact is only ever raw material, and everyone cuts it in their own way.`,
    `As a police report. In the year two thousand and twenty-four, on the twelfth of November, at eight forty-seven a.m., we, a sworn officer of the transport company, did observe that an individual of the male sex, hereinafter referred to as the respondent, took possession of an object of the umbrella type, black in colour, belonging to the complainant, hereinafter referred to as Mme D., and did so in lieu of a strictly identical object, black in colour, belonging to himself. The complainant declares she suffered no harm, other than moral and capillary. The respondent declares: "They looked so alike." The two parties having of their own accord returned the disputed objects, there are no grounds for further action. Duly recorded.`,
    `In the précieux manner. What shall I tell you, Madame? That morning the sky was pouring on Bordeaux the tears of an inconsolable Dawn, and everyone had furnished themselves with that portable canopy the common people call an umbrella. Our hero, yielding to the haste that is the scourge of modern souls, seized the shelter of a beauty whose very name he did not know, and left his own as a hostage. She, far from taking offence, pursued the abductor through the streams of the Intendance, and no one knows which prevailed in that race, outraged virtue or curiosity. It is said they surrendered their arms to each other with a courtesy that would have done honour to the court.`,
    `Live from the cours de l'Intendance. And they're off, ladies and gentlemen, slippery road, very difficult conditions! Diop is out of the tram, she's got the wrong umbrella, she knows it, she's heading back south! She overtakes a delivery rider, she passes a group of Spanish tourists on the outside, what acceleration! Up ahead, the gentleman doesn't see it coming, he's stopped at a bookshop window, a huge tactical mistake! Ten metres, five metres, she reaches out... contact! Umbrella exchange! And look at that, they're laughing! In twenty years of commentating, I've never seen anything like it!`,
    `As a horoscope. Libra, and you were born on 12 October, Monsieur Thibault Marchetti: a morning mix-up will put you in a delicate position. Venus, in transit on line B, invites you to return what does not belong to you; you will receive more in exchange. Avoid hasty decisions when approaching automatic doors. Health: watch out for colds. Colour of the day: black. Lucky accessory: you know which one.`,
    `By the umbrella itself. And now, if I may be allowed to speak, I who was at the heart of the affair without anyone thinking to question me: I was not taken by mistake. I threw myself. For three weeks, every Tuesday, I had seen the same man, standing by the door, reading over my owner's shoulder the novel she was reading, and turning his head when she turned the page. Three weeks in which neither of them dared say anything. Three weeks of rain. My twin and I, hanging from the same bar, exchanged a look, as far as umbrellas can, and agreed on a plan. As the doors were closing, I slipped my handle into his hand. The rest was a matter of courage, and I should like to make clear that it was not they who showed it.`,
    `Postscript, without manner. They are getting married in June, at the town hall in the centre. They have ordered sunshine. The two umbrellas have asked to be witnesses.`,
  ],
  "fr-c2-le-miroir-en-avance": [
    `I bought it at Drouot, one rainy Thursday, for a sum so modest that I should have been suspicious. It was an eighteenth-century Venetian mirror, its gilded wooden frame eaten away in places, its silvering speckled with grey spots like an old lady's skin. The auctioneer knocked it down with a strange haste, without even waiting for the room to finish raising their hands, and I thought I saw a man in the back row cross himself.`,
    `I hung it that same evening in my shop on the rue Mouffetard, between a Louis-Philippe chest of drawers and a lot of engravings. It was while tidying my hair before closing that I first noticed it: my reflection raised its hand to its hair a little before I did. A fraction of a second, no more. I put it down to tiredness, to the lighting, to the old glass, which rippled slightly. The next day the lead was a whole second. The day after, ten.`,
    `Within a week I had to face the facts: the mirror did not reflect the present but the one to come, and each day from a little further away. I saw myself, an hour ahead, wrapping a vase I did not yet know I had sold; I saw myself, a whole afternoon in advance, spilling tea on my ledger. I learned to use it as a weather forecast for my existence. I was not sorry, I confess, that it gave me an advantage over fate that no one else had.`,
    `Then the gap widened faster. After a month the mirror was a week ahead; after two, a year. I saw the shop change in it: the wallpaper being redone, the chest of drawers sold, a lamp I did not recognise. Above all I saw a man. Tall, slightly stooped, his fingers stained with glue, he came into the shop, sat by the stove, made me laugh. From his tools I understood that he was a bookbinder. I saw him kiss me, one snowy evening, reflected in the shop window. I spent the winter watching the door. Every time the bell tinkled, my heart leapt so absurdly that I was ashamed in front of the mirror.`,
    `By spring the mirror was ten years ahead. I watched myself grow old in it, white-haired, slower in my step. The man was still there, and then one day he was not. His chair stayed by the stove, empty, for weeks on end. I looked at that old woman on the other side of the glass, who spent her days staring at a chair, and I no longer knew which of us was the other's reflection.`,
    `I resolved to break the mirror. I was not afraid of what it might still show me; I was afraid that, having seen it, I could never live it. For I had noticed something terrible: since I had known this man in advance, I had been waiting for him the way one waits for a train whose timetable one knows, without surprise, without joy, almost impatient for it to have already gone by. Announced happiness was nothing more than an appointment; announced grief, a due date. It would have been better if I had known nothing.`,
    `On the morning I took down the mirror, wrapped in a sheet, to carry it down to the cellar with a hammer, the bell tinkled. A tall man, slightly stooped, his fingers stained with glue, stood in the doorway with a bound book under his arm. Pointing at the sheet, he asked if it was a painting. I said no, it was nothing, just an old thing, a mirror that only told you what you always end up knowing.`,
    `He came back the next day, and the day after. The mirror is still in the cellar under its sheet, and I have not broken it. As for the chair by the stove, I don't know. I have decided not to know any more. It is, I think, the only way I have found to be happy in the present: by having no lead on it.`,
  ],
  "fr-c2-la-prete-plume": [
    `From: Geneviève Lacasse. To: Marc-André Bélanger. Subject: chapter 1, first draft. Hello Marc-André, please find attached the first chapter of your memoirs. I followed our interviews, taking a few liberties with the structure. You'll see that I open on the skating rink of your childhood in Chicoutimi, one February morning at thirty below: a little boy skates alone under the stars before school and swears to the sky that one day he will play at the Bell Centre. Let me know if the tone suits you. G.L.`,
    `From: Marc-André. Subject: RE: chapter 1. Geneviève, I read it in the car this morning and I bawled like a baby at a red light. It's darn beautiful. Just one thing: I never skated alone under the stars. We didn't have a rink, we had the church car park, which my uncle flooded on the nights he was sober, which is to say not often. And I didn't swear much of anything to the sky, I was too cold. But leave it like that. It's better like that. M.-A.`,
    `From: Geneviève. Subject: chapters 2 to 4. Marc-André, here's what comes next. I took the liberty of fleshing out the figure of your father. You told me he worked nights at the Arvida aluminium smelter; I've made him a taciturn man who, after his shifts, drove you to every game in his old pickup truck without saying a word and waited for you in the cold until the final buzzer. I've also added a scene where your mother sews your first number onto a sweater that's too big, the night before your first tournament. Readers need a father and a mother. So does the book. G.`,
    `From: Marc-André. Subject: RE: chapters 2 to 4. My father came to one single game. One. I counted. But that's fine, keep the truck. I told the truck story on the show yesterday, and the host had tears in his eyes. People love that. My girlfriend says I've started talking like a book. I like that too, I think. M.-A.`,
    `From: Marc-André. Subject: (no subject). Sent at 3:12 a.m. Geneviève, my mother called me. She heard the show. She asked me what truck I was talking about, and what rink, and where the stars were. She wasn't angry. She sounded worried, as if I'd lost my memory. I didn't answer her. I'm going to tell you the real thing, the one I didn't tell you in the interviews. My father came to one game, in 1989, in Jonquière. I was twelve. I missed a penalty shot in the third period and we lost. In the car on the way home he said nothing for twenty minutes, and then, as we got to the bridge, he said: "You tried. It's OK." That's all. He died the following winter. That's what I want in the book. Not the stars.`,
    `From: Geneviève. Subject: new version. Marc-André, I've rewritten everything. There are no more stars. There's a church car park, an uncle sober one night in three, a mother mending sweaters, a missed penalty shot in Jonquière and a man who says nothing for twenty minutes before finding the two truest sentences of your life. It's less beautiful. It's better. I suggest a title: "It's OK". And I owe you a confession: it took me eleven years in this job to understand that you don't embellish a life, you politely betray it. G.`,
    `From: Marc-André. Subject: RE: new version. The title is perfect. My mother read the first chapter; she said it really was me. One last thing and then I'll leave you alone: I want your name on the cover, next to mine. Not in small print, not on the inside. You didn't lend me your pen. You gave me back mine, and that gets signed by two people. And tell your publisher it's not negotiable. M.-A.`,
  ],
  "fr-c2-il-eut-fallu-que-vous-vinssiez": [
    `Maître Bertrand Lamoureux-Tissier, honorary notary in Dijon, sixty-seven years old, widowed for six, had three passions: the great wines of the Côte de Nuits, Rameau's harpsichord pieces and the imperfect subjunctive. The first two had earned him the esteem of his peers; the third, an almost complete solitude. His late wife, Mireille, had ended up getting used to it the way one gets used to the chimes of a nearby church; his clients, for their part, signed faster than elsewhere, to cut things short. It is difficult, he observed without bitterness, to order a kir at the bar while specifying that one would have wished it to be served less cold.`,
    `It was his daughter, at the end of her tether, who signed him up for a speed-dating evening at a wine bar near the place de la Libération: seven minutes per table, a bell, you move on. For the occasion he chose a claret-coloured tie, which, for a Burgundian, bordered on provocation. "Papa, promise me one thing: talk normally." "I fail to see, my darling, that there was ever any question of my speaking otherwise."`,
    `The first lady, a pharmacist from Beaune, lasted four minutes. He had confided that he would have liked her to tell him about her travels; she thought he was reproaching her for not having done so and, to defend herself, told him about her ex-husband. The second, a yoga teacher, found him very authentic and asked which theatre he acted in. The third got up after ninety seconds, when he had the misfortune to say he would not have believed one could live happily in Chalon-sur-Saône. She was from Chalon, and told him so in terms that grammar frowns upon.`,
    `He was beginning to wonder whether he ought to give up when the bell rang for the fourth table. A woman of his age, with short grey hair and work-worn hands, sat down opposite him and set on the table a glass of red she had brought herself. She introduced herself: Solange Morot, winegrower in Fixin, forty harvests. He introduced himself. She studied him for a moment, with the attention one gives to a wine that might be corked.`,
    `"Your daughter warned me," she said. "She's at the table at the back, keeping an eye on things. She told me: my father's going to talk to you like a book from 1850, be kind to him." Bertrand blushed to the tips of his ears. He stammered that he would have preferred her not to know. Solange put her glass down. "But, my dear sir, you should have come sooner. I have been waiting thirty years for someone to speak to me like that. My late husband conjugated everything in the present, even reproaches."`,
    `What followed is still told by witnesses in the wine cellars of the Côte. For seven minutes, then for the twenty-eight that followed, since the bell no longer had any effect on them, they engaged in a duel of unheard-of elegance. One by one the other participants abandoned their tables; the organiser, a young woman armed with a stopwatch, ended up sitting at the bar to listen to them. He wished her to describe her vineyards; she demanded that he recount his most scandalous estates. He regretted that they had not met sooner; she retorted that they must not complain: at their age, one no longer waited for things to happen, one made them happen. Towards the end he dared: he would be happy if she agreed to dine with him on Saturday. She took the time to taste her wine. "It would have been a shame for me to refuse."`,
    `At the table at the back, the daughter texted her brother: "That's it. There are two of them." The following Saturday, at the restaurant, the waiter took their order with growing unease. They have dined together every Saturday since. It is said that they now speak in the present tense when they are alone, and that they reserve the imperfect subjunctive for their quarrels, which it makes, Solange assures us, much shorter: by the time you've found the right ending, you've forgotten why you were angry.`,
  ],
  "fr-c2-memoires-d-un-chat-de-montparnasse": [
    `I undertake these Memoirs at the age of eleven, which among my kind is equivalent to the most accomplished maturity, and I undertake them because I have seen, in the brief span of one reign, more revolutions than an honest cat ought to see in nine lives. I was named Talleyrand by the Sovereign, Mme Linh Nguyen, owner of the Café des Quatre-Vents on the boulevard du Montparnasse; the name pleased me; I have borne its dignity ever since without faltering, and most often on the counter.`,
    `My court was small, but select. The first of the regulars was an old poet named M. Abdelkader, a man of merit, of little fortune, of old-fashioned courtesy, who gave me half of his croissant every morning: I have never known a greater lord. Next came the waiter Florian, a well-made lad of pleasant face and short wit, who pushed me aside with his foot to get past, which I have forgiven him, but not forgotten. The Sovereign herself, a woman of great good sense and a firm hand, governed the till, the coffee machine and everyone's moods with an evenness I have rarely observed in princes. The others do not deserve to be dwelt upon: they were students, tourists and people who work on computers, a numerous species with no conversation.`,
    `It was on a Tuesday in March that there appeared the one I shall call, for want of knowing a name that would distinguish him, the Aspirant. He was round, flat, slate-grey, and had neither eyes nor ears; he moved about on his own, purring in a manner I immediately judged outrageous, for purring is a privilege, and there is not a creature in the kingdom who has received it without earning it. The Sovereign presented him to the room with a tenderness I had never seen her show to anyone but me.`,
    `He rose with a speed that confounds. Within a week he had his hours, his privileges of access, his corner by the radiator, which was mine, and a little plastic house to which he retired, it was said, to recharge, as ministers do. Florian talked to him. The customers photographed him. The old poet himself, whom I had believed immune to flattery, devoted a sonnet to him. I learned then what disgrace is: my croissant was served to me whole, but without tenderness, and on a saucer.`,
    `I resolved to bring about his ruin. I shall not report the details of my manoeuvres, which are not all to my credit. I laid traps of balls of wool for him, in which he choked twice; I knocked a glass of mint diabolo into his path, for which I was suspected without anything being proved; I slept three nights on his little house so that he could not get back in. He held firm. He had over me that terrible advantage of soulless beings, which is never to lose heart, and this other one, more terrible still, of never noticing that one hates him.`,
    `The denouement came from where I least expected it. One bitterly cold evening, the Sovereign, while closing, forgot to switch off the Aspirant, who wandered for a long time in the dark room and came to die, I mean to come to a halt, against my basket. I noticed that he was warm. I noticed further that he was exactly the width of my person. I sat on him, out of curiosity and without the slightest intention of forgiving, and what no courtier would have dared imagine came to pass: he set off again, and he took me for a ride.`,
    `Since then I can be seen every morning making a tour of the room, carried at a gentle pace on the Aspirant, with the gravity that rank demands and that the customers, who know nothing, mistake for laziness. They film me; I am assured that I am famous in countries I shall never visit. The poet has written a second sonnet, dedicated to me. I have understood, late in life, the great lesson of courts: one does not overthrow a favourite; one climbs on top of him.`,
  ],
};

